const body = `<h1>Student RPG Guide: Dice Roller</h1>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>This is YOUR guide.</strong> Every dice roll, damage calculation, and loot
    drop in your RPG depends on this class. Get it right once, use it all year. Instead of
    giving you the code, this guide provides the <strong>Acceptance Criteria</strong>. It is your
    job to write the code that satisfies the criteria.
</div>

<h2>The Setup</h2>

<p>Create two files in your project:</p>
<ol>
    <li><code>Dice.java</code> — This will contain all your dice logic.</li>
    <li><code>DiceTester.java</code> — This will contain a <code>main</code> method to run your own tests.</li>
</ol>

<p>In <code>Dice.java</code>, start with a basic class outline. You will add methods to this class.</p>
<pre><code>public class Dice {
    // Your methods will go here
}</code></pre>

<p>
    There is a <strong>third</strong> file, and you do not write it:
    <a href="../../data/DiceCheck.java" download>DiceCheck.java</a>. It is an
    acceptance test. Download it and put it in the same folder as your
    <code>Dice.java</code> — the next section shows how to run it.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>How to Run the Acceptance Test</h2>

<p>
    <code>DiceCheck.java</code> calls every method in this guide and prints
    <code>PASS</code> or <code>FAIL</code> for each one. It does not read your code —
    it <em>runs</em> it, so the only way to pass is to actually be correct.
</p>

<ol>
    <li>
        Download <a href="../../data/DiceCheck.java" download>DiceCheck.java</a> and
        put it in the same folder as your <code>Dice.java</code>.
    </li>
    <li>Open a terminal in that folder.</li>
    <li>
        Compile both files together:
        <pre><code>javac Dice.java DiceCheck.java</code></pre>
        Both must compile before anything runs. If the error message names one of
        your methods, that method's signature does not match the spec below — fix
        the signature and compile again.
    </li>
    <li>
        Run the tests:
        <pre><code>java DiceCheck</code></pre>
    </li>
    <li>
        Read the output. Every check is a <code>PASS</code> or a <code>FAIL</code>,
        and the last two lines count them:
        <pre><code>32 passed, 0 failed
Your Dice engine meets the spec.</code></pre>
    </li>
</ol>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>If the compile step fails on everything at once</strong>, check the file
    names before you check the logic. <code>javac</code> is case-sensitive, and the
    class inside <code>Dice.java</code> must be named <code>Dice</code> — not
    <code>dice</code>, not <code>DiceRoller</code>.
</div>

<p>
    <strong>Why ranges, and never exact numbers?</strong> Dice are random, so a correct
    <code>roll(20)</code> is allowed to return anything from 1 to 20. No test can demand
    a particular value. Instead the test rolls each method thousands of times and checks
    the <em>range</em> of what comes back. A d6 that can return 0 fails, and a
    <code>roll(3, 6)</code> that can return 19 fails, no matter how the individual rolls
    happened to land.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Part 1: roll() — Roll a d6</h2>

<h3>Requirements:</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Method signature: <code>public static int roll()</code></li>
    <li>Behavior: Rolls one standard six-sided die. Returns an integer from 1 to 6, inclusive.</li>
    <li>This is a <em>convenience overload</em> — it should call <code>roll(6)</code>, not repeat the random formula.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Socratic Checkpoint:</h3>
<p>Why call <code>roll(6)</code> instead of writing the <code>Math.random()</code> formula a second time? If you later discover a bug in that formula, how many places would you have to fix?</p>

<h3>Acceptance Test:</h3>
<pre><code>System.out.println("Rolling a d6: " + Dice.roll());</code></pre>
<p>Run it several times. Do you ever see a 0? A 7? A negative number?</p>

<hr>

<h2>Part 2: roll(int sides) — Roll Any Die</h2>

<h3>Requirements:</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Method signature: <code>public static int roll(int sides)</code></li>
    <li>Behavior: Rolls one die with the given number of <code>sides</code>. Returns an integer from 1 to <code>sides</code>, inclusive.</li>
    <li>This is the one method that actually contains the <code>Math.random()</code> formula. Every other method in this class calls it.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Socratic Checkpoint:</h3>
<p>Look back at your <code>Math.random()</code> lab notebook. What is the "range" here, based on the <code>sides</code> parameter? What is the "min"? And what does <code>(int) Math.random() * sides</code> return instead — where do the parentheses belong?</p>

<h3>Acceptance Test:</h3>
<pre><code>System.out.println("Rolling d20: " + Dice.roll(20));
System.out.println("Rolling d6:  " + Dice.roll(6));
System.out.println("Rolling d2:  " + Dice.roll(2));</code></pre>
<p>Run it many times. Can <code>roll(2)</code> ever return a 3? Can <code>roll(20)</code> ever return 0?</p>

<hr>

<h2>Part 3: roll(int num, int sides) — Roll Several Dice and Add Them Up</h2>

<h3>Requirements:</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Method signature: <code>public static int roll(int num, int sides)</code></li>
    <li>Behavior: Rolls <code>num</code> dice, each with <code>sides</code>, and returns the total. <code>roll(2, 6)</code> is 2d6, so it returns 2 through 12.</li>
    <li>Call your own <code>roll(sides)</code> once per die. Do not write the random formula again.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Tinker Challenge:</h3>
<p>
    You need to add up a new roll <code>num</code> times. A <em>loop</em> is the tool
    for "do this a fixed number of times", and you will meet it properly later this
    year. Here is the shape — read it before you use it:
</p>
<pre><code>int total = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; num; i++) {
    total = total + roll(sides);
}
return total;</code></pre>
<p>What is <code>i</code> for? Why does the loop start at 0 and stop <em>before</em> <code>num</code>? What would <code>i &lt;= num</code> do to a 2d6 roll?</p>

<h3>Acceptance Test:</h3>
<pre><code>System.out.println("Rolling 3d6: " + Dice.roll(3, 6));
System.out.println("Rolling 2d6: " + Dice.roll(2, 6));</code></pre>
<p>
    Run it. Can <code>roll(3, 6)</code> return below 3? Above 18? And can
    <code>roll(2, 6)</code> return 7 — an odd number? It should be able to, because
    3 + 4 is a perfectly legal pair of dice. If every 2d6 result you ever see is
    even, you are multiplying by <code>num</code> instead of adding <code>num</code>
    dice together.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Part 4: roll(int num, int sides, int stat) — Add an Ability Modifier</h2>

<h3>Requirements:</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Method signature: <code>public static int roll(int num, int sides, int stat)</code></li>
    <li>Behavior: Rolls <code>num</code> dice of <code>sides</code> as in Part 3, then adds the modifier for the ability score <code>stat</code>.</li>
    <li>The modifier is <code>(stat - 10) / 2</code>. A strength of 14 gives a +2 bonus, a strength of 10 gives no change, and a strength of 8 gives a -1 penalty.</li>
    <li>Call <code>roll(num, sides)</code>. Do not repeat the loop.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Socratic Checkpoint:</h3>
<p><code>stat</code> arrives as an ability score like 14, but the roll needs a small bonus like +2. Where does the <code>- 10</code> come from, and why divide by 2? Work out the modifier for a strength of 10, then for 20, before you write any code.</p>

<h3>Acceptance Test:</h3>
<pre><code>System.out.println("2d6 + STR(14): " + Dice.roll(2, 6, 14));  // 4 to 14
System.out.println("2d6 + STR(10): " + Dice.roll(2, 6, 10));  // 2 to 12
System.out.println("2d6 + STR(8):  " + Dice.roll(2, 6, 8));   // 1 to 11</code></pre>
<p>Run each one many times and check the results against the range in the comment. If <code>STR(14)</code> ever prints a 2, your dice are not being rolled. If <code>STR(8)</code> ever prints a 12, your modifier is not being applied.</p>

<h3>Tinker Challenge: the odd-score trap</h3>
<p>
    Try a strength of 9: <code>Dice.roll(2, 6, 9)</code>. By the D&amp;D rulebook a 9
    should give a -1 penalty, because the true midpoint between 0 and -1 rounds down.
    What does your formula actually give? Java's integer division drops the remainder
    by moving toward zero, so <code>-1 / 2</code> is <code>0</code>, not
    <code>-1</code>. Odd scores below 10 are the only ones affected: 3, 5, 7, and 9.
</p>
<p>
    Two ways out. Leave it, and a 9 behaves like a 10. Or reach for
    <code>Math.floorDiv(stat - 10, 2)</code>, which always rounds down and matches the
    rulebook exactly. Either is acceptable here — but decide deliberately, and write
    down which you chose and why.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Part 5: skillCheck(int pass) — Roll a d20 Against a Difficulty</h2>

<h3>Requirements:</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Method signature: <code>public static int skillCheck(int pass)</code></li>
    <li>Behavior: Rolls a d20 and compares it to <code>pass</code>, the difficulty to beat. Returns an <code>int</code> code, not a boolean, so the caller can tell a lucky win from a brilliant one.</li>
</ul>

<table>
    <tr><th>Outcome</th><th>Code</th><th>When</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Crit fail</td><td>-1</td><td>The die shows a natural 1</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Fail</td><td>0</td><td>The roll is below <code>pass</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Success</td><td>1</td><td>The roll is at least <code>pass</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Crit success</td><td>2</td><td>The die shows a natural 20</td></tr>
</table>

<p>
    Check the natural 1 and the natural 20 <strong>first</strong>, before you compare
    anything to <code>pass</code>. A natural 1 is a crit fail even when the difficulty
    was only 2, and a natural 20 is a crit success even when the difficulty was
    impossible.
</p>

<h3>Socratic Checkpoint:</h3>
<p>Does the table above need an <code>if</code>, an <code>if/else</code>, or an <code>if/else if/else</code> chain? Count how many distinct outcomes there are before you answer.</p>

<h3>Acceptance Test:</h3>
<pre><code>System.out.println("DC 15: " + Dice.skillCheck(15));
System.out.println("DC  1: " + Dice.skillCheck(1));   // only a natural 1 fails
System.out.println("DC 21: " + Dice.skillCheck(21));  // only a natural 20 passes</code></pre>
<p>
    Run it a few times and watch the edge cases, not the middle. With DC 1 you should
    never see a 0 — every die is at least 1, so only the natural 1 stops you, and that
    is coded -1. With DC 21 you should never see a 1 — no die can reach 21, so the best
    you can do is the natural 20.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Part 6: skillCheck(int pass, int skill) — Add an Ability Modifier</h2>

<h3>Requirements:</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Method signature: <code>public static int skillCheck(int pass, int skill)</code></li>
    <li>Behavior: Rolls a d20, adds the modifier for the ability score <code>skill</code>, and compares the total to <code>pass</code>. Same four codes as Part 5.</li>
    <li>You call it like <code>Dice.skillCheck(14, str)</code> — 14 is the difficulty of the check, and <code>str</code> is your strength score.</li>
    <li>The natural 1 and natural 20 are still read off the <em>die</em>, before the modifier. A +5 strength does not rescue a natural 1.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Socratic Checkpoint:</h3>
<p><code>skillCheck(14, str)</code> needs the modifier for <code>str</code>. You already worked out that arithmetic in Part 4 — will you be calling something that does it, or typing <code>(str - 10) / 2</code> a second time?</p>

<h3>Acceptance Test:</h3>
<pre><code>System.out.println("DC 14, STR 14 (+2): " + Dice.skillCheck(14, 14));
System.out.println("DC 14, STR 10 (+0): " + Dice.skillCheck(14, 10));
System.out.println("DC 14, STR 18 (+4): " + Dice.skillCheck(14, 18));</code></pre>
<p>Run each line twenty times. The <code>STR 18</code> line should succeed noticeably more often than the <code>STR 10</code> line. If all three look about the same, your modifier is not reaching the roll — and a test with a difficulty of 24 will catch it.</p>

<hr>

<h2>Part 7: statRoll(int stats) — Generate a Whole Set of Ability Scores</h2>

<h3>Requirements:</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Method signature: <code>public static int[] statRoll(int stats)</code></li>
    <li>Behavior: Returns a <strong>new array</strong> of length <code>stats</code>. Each element is a 3d6 roll, so each one is between 3 and 18.</li>
    <li><code>statRoll(6)</code> fills six stats — STR, DEX, CON, INT, WIS, CHA. <code>statRoll(4)</code> fills four.</li>
    <li>Call <code>roll(3, 6)</code> for each element. Do not repeat the loop or the formula.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Tinker Challenge:</h3>
<p>A new <code>int</code> array starts full of zeros. You need to replace each zero with a fresh 3d6 roll — one roll per index:</p>
<pre><code>int[] scores = new int[stats];
for (int i = 0; i &lt; stats; i++) {
    scores[i] = roll(3, 6);
}
return scores;</code></pre>
<p>What is the length of the array when <code>stats</code> is 6? What is the index of its last element? Why is <code>i &lt; stats</code> correct where <code>i &lt;= stats</code> would crash?</p>

<h3>Acceptance Test:</h3>
<pre><code>int[] stats = Dice.statRoll(6);
String[] names = {"STR", "DEX", "CON", "INT", "WIS", "CHA"};
for (int i = 0; i &lt; stats.length; i++) {
    System.out.println(names[i] + ": " + stats[i]);
}</code></pre>
<p>Expected: six labeled numbers, each between 3 and 18. Run it several times — do the numbers move around? If every stat is the same number, you are filling the array with one roll instead of rolling per index.</p>`;
